# qumo relay — environment variable configuration example
#
# Usage:
#   export $(grep -v '^#' relay.env | xargs)   # bash/zsh
#   qumo relay
#
# For systemd deployments, use:
#   EnvironmentFile=/etc/qumo/relay.env
#
# For Docker:
#   docker run --env-file relay.env ghcr.io/qumo-dev/qumo relay

# ─── Server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Address to bind the relay server (QUIC/MoQT).
RELAY_ADDR=0.0.0.0:4433

# TLS certificate and key (required — the relay exits at startup if it can't
# load them). Generate a dev cert with mkcert:
#   mkcert -cert-file certs/server.crt -key-file certs/server.key localhost 127.0.0.1 ::1
CERT_FILE=certs/server.crt
KEY_FILE=certs/server.key

# ─── Graceful migration / GOAWAY (optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# GOAWAY is an escape-hatch mobility primitive. Route/subscription migration
# (make-before-break via route election + Drainable) is the primary mechanism;
# set this only when you want graceful shutdown to redirect clients/peers to a
# successor relay (gomoqt Server.NextSessionURI, sent on Shutdown).
# GOAWAY_REDIRECT_URI=https://successor.example.com:4433

# ─── mTLS (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PEM CA certificate. When set, mutual TLS is enabled:
#   - incoming peer connections that present a cert are verified against this CA;
#   - the dialer presents this node's CERT_FILE cert to remote relays and
#     trusts only the CA pool;
#   - remote resolver clients also present the client cert and verify the
#     remote server against this CA (when REMOTE_TLS_ENABLED=true).
# Leave unset to disable mTLS entirely (default). Once CA_FILE is set, every
# connection MUST present a client cert signed by it, UNLESS you relax that
# with MTLS_REQUIRED=false below.
# CA_FILE=certs/ca.crt

# Set to false to allow connections without a client cert once mTLS is enabled
# (cert is verified if presented, missing cert is still allowed — use this if
# the relay also serves direct browser/WebTransport traffic).
# Default: true (every connection must present a cert signed by CA_FILE) —
# use this for relay-only clusters with no direct browser traffic.
# MTLS_REQUIRED=false

# ─── Node identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Human-readable node identifier (defaults to hostname).
RELAY_NAME=relay-tokyo

# NOTE: the node topology role is a flag, not an env var — set it on the command:
#   qumo relay --role hub      # or "edge"; omit for a standalone relay.

# ─── Capacity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Number of completed groups each track's ring retains for late/backfill
# subscribers (default: 8). Raise to absorb longer subscriber startup lag at
# the cost of per-track memory.
# GROUP_CACHE_SIZE=8

# Frame buffer capacity in bytes (default: 1500, roughly one network MTU).
# FRAME_CAPACITY=1500

# ─── Networking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# UDP receive buffer size (SO_RCVBUF) for the relay's single QUIC listener
# socket, in bytes. At high fan-out (thousands of concurrent subscribers
# connecting in burst), the default OS receive buffer (~8 KB on Windows,
# ~208 KB on Linux) can overflow, dropping QUIC Initial packets and causing
# connection failures.
#
# The relay defaults to 262144 (256 KB) regardless of OS, which is comfortably
# above both Windows and Linux defaults. Increase further (e.g. 2097152 for
# 2 MB) on large deployments pushing >15K concurrent sessions.
#
# NOTE (Linux): the kernel silently caps SO_RCVBUF at net.core.rmem_max and does
# NOT error when the request exceeds it — so a value above rmem_max is quietly
# clamped, and the logged size reflects the request, not the applied buffer.
# To actually get e.g. 2 MB, raise the limit first:
#   sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=2097152   (persist in /etc/sysctl.d/)
# Set to 0 to disable the override and use the unmodified OS default.
# RELAY_UDP_RCVBUF=262144

# ─── Profiling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Opt-in net/http/pprof endpoints on the relay's HTTP port (/debug/pprof/...),
# alongside /metrics. Off by default — pprof exposes runtime internals (heap
# object graphs, goroutine stacks), so enable it only on a trusted/loopback
# interface. Useful for capturing CPU/heap profiles under load (e.g. while
# qumo loadgen drives the relay to its session ceiling).
# RELAY_PPROF=1

# ─── CORS — WebTransport origin check ────────────────────────────────────────

# Origins permitted to open WebTransport sessions to the relay (qumo relay) and
# the standalone ingest servers (qumo rtmp / qumo rtsp). Comma-separated. Special
# entries: "*" allows any origin; "same-host" allows any port on the request's
# own host (the default qumo playground uses, since it serves the UI and relay on
# the same host at different ports). If unset, only same-origin and headerless
# (non-browser) clients are accepted — set this when the UI is served from a
# different origin than the server (e.g. a Vite dev server on another port).
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,http://localhost:5178

# ─── Static peers (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated list of peer relay addresses.
# The node connects to each peer and relays their announcements.
# PEERS=moqt://relay-london.example.com:4433,moqt://relay-newyork.example.com:4433

# ─── Upstream connection (edge / hierarchical relays) ───────────────────────

# Address of upstream relay(s) to connect to (comma-separated).
# Used by edge relays to stream announcements to/from regional hub relays.
# Supports Consul DNS service discovery or direct host:port addresses.
# UPSTREAM_ADDR=role-hub.qumo-relay.service.consul:4433

# ─── Credential auth & metering (optional) ───────────────────────────────────

# Base URL of the qumo credential server. When set, the relay:
#   - authenticates publisher JWTs via POST /v1/credentials/introspect;
#   - reports cumulative ingress/egress byte totals via POST /v1/usage/events.
# Leave unset to disable credential auth and metering (open relay mode).
# QUMO_CREDENTIAL_URL=https://credentials.example.com

# Shared bearer token that the relay presents to the credential server.
# Must match the QUMO_RELAY_TOKEN configured on the server.
# QUMO_RELAY_TOKEN=supersecret

# --- GC tuning (high-fan-out capacity) ---
# The relay holds a large, stable live set: one QUIC connection per subscriber,
# whose goroutine stacks dominate RSS. Every GC cycle re-scans those stacks, so
# at high session counts GC-scan CPU becomes the scaling ceiling. Raising the GC
# target percentage collects the (legitimate, non-garbage) live set less often,
# trading peak RSS for markedly less GC CPU.
#
# This is OPT-IN: if neither GOGC nor RELAY_GOGC is set, the relay leaves the Go
# runtime default (100) untouched. GOGC (the runtime's own env) always wins if
# set and is never overridden. Set RELAY_GOGC to raise the target on big-memory
# hosts pushing >15K concurrent subscriber sessions: RELAY_GOGC=600..1600 reached
# ~18-20K sessions on an 8-core host in testing. Do NOT use GOMEMLIMIT for this
# workload — capping memory forces constant GC and collapses throughput.
# RELAY_GOGC=800
#
# Run `qumo doctor` to see the effective GC target, which input won and why, and
# guidance — read-only, it changes nothing.
